WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.958 --> 00:00:11.781
OLIVIA: Welcome to this week's People in the News ! Today we're going to introduce you to

2
00:00:11.783 --> 00:00:15.636
Angela Merkel, the first female chancellor of Germany. She is often called the most powerful woman in

3
00:00:15.637 --> 00:00:24.874
the world. BEN: That’s true, Olivia. Even though Angela is the political leader of Germany, she's also steered

4
00:00:24.875 --> 00:00:29.109
the rest of Europe through a very challenging economic period. Angela was born in Hamburg, West Germany,

5
00:00:29.110 --> 00:00:33.606
in 1954. Her father was a Lutheran minister and her mother taught Latin and English. When Angela

6
00:00:33.607 --> 00:00:40.765
was still a baby, her family moved to East Germany so that her father could serve in

7
00:00:40.766 --> 00:00:46.921
a congregation there. According to her friends, Angela often seemed eager to prove that she could accomplish

8
00:00:46.922 --> 00:00:56.488
difficult tasks. This led her to become a top student—and dream about becoming a figure skater one day,

9
00:00:56.489 --> 00:01:04.075
even though she couldn’t even walk across a balance beam without falling! And when she headed to

10
00:01:04.076 --> 00:01:10.337
college, she decided to study physics for a similar reason; it was the only subject she had

11
00:01:10.338 --> 00:01:14.237
ever flunked. Like many other East German children, Angela joined the Communist youth movement, the Free German

12
00:01:14.238 --> 00:01:19.331
Youth. But the main reason she joined was to avoid standing out. The last thing she wanted was

13
00:01:19.332 --> 00:01:25.074
for the East German government to notice her or her family! Back then East Germany was part

14
00:01:25.075 --> 00:01:31.645
of the Eastern bloc, a group of Communist states. The government was very oppressive, censoring the news

15
00:01:31.646 --> 00:01:37.301
and even building a huge wall through the city of Berlin. They did this to stop citizens

16
00:01:37.302 --> 00:01:42.306
from fleeing to West Germany, where people had a lot more freedom. The East German government also

17
00:01:42.307 --> 00:01:50.052
created a secret police force called the Stasi. The Stasi kept track of what citizens were doing by

18
00:01:50.053 --> 00:01:55.550
planting spies everywhere—factories, hospitals, schools. The Stasi listened to people’s phone conversations and placed hidden cameras in

19
00:01:55.551 --> 00:02:01.286
their homes. They also kidnapped hundreds of East Germans who had left the country, and then executed

20
00:02:01.287 --> 00:02:07.031
them. OLIVIA: Wow . . . Growing up in a place like that must have been really

21
00:02:07.032 --> 00:02:15.865
hard. BEN: It made Angela cautious and deliberate, someone who carefully considers decisions. It also taught her

22
00:02:15.866 --> 00:02:21.856
to deeply value privacy and freedom. After college Angela moved to Berlin, to work as a chemist at

23
00:02:21.857 --> 00:02:26.092
the German Academy of Sciences. While she was there, she earned a doctorate degree in quantum chemistry.

24
00:02:26.093 --> 00:02:35.577
Then, in 1989, something amazing happened in Berlin. This event changed East Germany forever, and it also

25
00:02:35.578 --> 00:02:42.417
transformed Angela. By the late 1980s, people were putting a lot of pressure on the East German

26
00:02:42.418 --> 00:02:48.503
government to tear down the Berlin Wall. The Eastern bloc had weakened, and citizens were protesting with

27
00:02:48.504 --> 00:02:55.987
peaceful acts of civil disobedience. At last, on November 9, 1989, the East German government announced that the

28
00:02:55.988 --> 00:03:00.898
Berlin Wall had "fallen." This meant that East Germans could now legally cross the border into West

29
00:03:00.899 --> 00:03:07.992
Germany. It was an exciting moment in history as people began climbing over the wall and even

30
00:03:07.993 --> 00:03:12.490
stealing bits of it for souvenirs. Angela was right there in Berlin, at the center of what

31
00:03:12.491 --> 00:03:16.732
was happening. OLIVIA: Is that why Angela decided to get involved in politics? BEN: Yes. The falling of

32
00:03:16.733 --> 00:03:22.881
the Berlin Wall inspired her to become involved in shaping the new Germany. She became a spokesperson

33
00:03:22.882 --> 00:03:32.542
for the political party Democratic Awakening, and after West and East Germany were reunified, more leadership positions

34
00:03:32.543 --> 00:03:44.213
for her followed. German Chancellor Helmut Kohl named her Minister of Family Affairs, Senior Citizens, Women, and

35
00:03:44.214 --> 00:03:52.873
Youth in 1991, and Minister of Environment, Conservation, and Reactor Safety in 1994. Then, when a financial

36
00:03:52.874 --> 00:03:58.038
scandal rocked Angela’s political party around 1999, she spoke out against her former mentor, Chancellor Kohl. This was

37
00:03:58.039 --> 00:04:04.925
a huge political risk for her, and many German people admired her for taking it. In 2005

38
00:04:04.926 --> 00:04:08.351
she was elected the first female chancellor of Germany and the first East German to hold this

39
00:04:08.352 --> 00:04:16.097
office. She was also the youngest chancellor ever elected. Angela was determined to revive Germany’s economy. She

40
00:04:16.098 --> 00:04:23.992
did this mostly by keeping taxes low and creating more jobs. She won many fans with her

41
00:04:23.993 --> 00:04:26.910
habits of working hard and attacking problems slowly, step-by-step, as a scientist would do. Lots of people also

42
00:04:26.911 --> 00:04:32.574
admired how simply she lived; she continued to live in her own apartment in Berlin and do

43
00:04:32.575 --> 00:04:40.813
her own shopping in local supermarkets. But while Germany’s economy was growing strong, other countries in Europe

44
00:04:40.814 --> 00:04:48.137
began struggling with a recession that brought high debt and high unemployment. Because Germany’s economy was healthy,

45
00:04:48.138 --> 00:04:55.479
Angela became a very powerful voice in the EU—the European Union. She pushed hard for cutting government

46
00:04:55.480 --> 00:05:00.881
spending and debt. She believed that was the best way for Europe to recover from the recession. Angela’s

47
00:05:00.882 --> 00:05:05.299
ideas received some strong criticism, especially from outside Germany. While some members of the EU agreed with

48
00:05:05.300 --> 00:05:10.297
Angela, other members disagreed; they thought people needed more help than ever from governments during tough economic

49
00:05:10.298 --> 00:05:19.783
times! These EU members also resented that Angela, a leader from another country, would be very involved

50
00:05:19.784 --> 00:05:26.933
in making economic decisions that affected their countries. In Germany Angela remains pretty popular. But she sometimes gets

51
00:05:26.934 --> 00:05:31.542
criticized for some really silly stuff. People make fun of her dowdy style of dress and stern

52
00:05:31.543 --> 00:05:37.103
public demeanor. While Angela may seem stern in public, her friends say that she is warm and

53
00:05:37.104 --> 00:05:43.283
funny in private. In fact, she’s really good at imitating people and often mimics the world leaders

54
00:05:43.284 --> 00:05:49.424
with whom she meets! Oh, and she’s a big fan of soccer and loves to attend the

55
00:05:49.425 --> 00:05:54.930
national German team’s games. In 2013 Angela was elected to a third term as Germany’s chancellor. OLIVIA: Thanks,

56
00:05:54.931 --> 00:06:04.101
Ben. Angela Merkel seems like a very smart and determined person—no wonder she’s called the most powerful

57
00:06:04.102 --> 00:06:11.586
woman in the world! See you soon, Little Fox readers, for another People in the News .

